---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import HeadlineMotion from "../components/HeadlineMotion.astro";
import CallActions from "../components/CallActions.astro";
import YardPlan from "../components/YardPlan.astro";
import PriceMath from "../components/PriceMath.astro";
import SampleEstimate from "../components/SampleEstimate.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How Yardulum works"
  description="Four steps from yard to itemized proposal. The property model works today; designing, pricing and the proposal are being built."
>
  <div class="container page">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">How It Works</p>
      <h1>
        <HeadlineMotion
          effect="rise"
          lines={["From their yard", "to the kitchen table."]}
        />
      </h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Four steps. The first works today. The other three are what we’re
        building now.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="step" aria-labelledby="step-1">
      <h2 id="step-1">1. Their yard, modeled</h2>
      <figure class="yd-step-fig">
        <div class="yd-figure">
          <YardPlan layer="model" id="yd-step1" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          Illustration of a sample yard, measured on the model.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>
          Before a visit, we build a 3D model of the property from free public
          mapping data: the ground, the house and the trees. Two taps on the
          model give the distance and the slope between any two points.
        </p>
        <p class="state">
          <strong>Works today.</strong> For now, we build each model for you,
          and Charles shows it to you in person.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="step section" aria-labelledby="step-2">
      <h2 id="step-2">2. Your design on it</h2>
      <figure class="yd-step-fig">
        <div class="yd-figure">
          <YardPlan layer="design" id="yd-step2" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Illustration. Pink marks new work.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>
          You’ll draw beds, edging, plants and patios on the model, using the
          items you sell. The quantities will come from what you draw.
        </p>
        <p class="state">
          <strong>Being built.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="step section" aria-labelledby="step-3">
      <h2 id="step-3">3. Your prices on every item</h2>
      <PriceMath />
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>
          Each item will be priced from your own price list, with labor hours.
          Change the design and the total will change with it. It’s plain
          arithmetic you can check, line by line.
        </p>
        <p class="state">
          <strong>Being built.</strong> The slope shows on the model, but
          Yardulum won’t price the slope for you. Grading will go on the
          estimate as its own line, from your price list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="step section" aria-labelledby="step-4">
      <h2 id="step-4">4. Closed at the table</h2>
      <figure class="yd-step-fig" data-sample>
        <div class="yd-figure yd-proposal-wrap">
          <SampleEstimate variant="proposal" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Illustration with sample prices. Not a quote.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="step-body">
        <p>
          You’ll walk your customer through their own yard, your design and
          every line of the price, then hand them a
          <span class="nobr">one-page</span> itemized proposal, ready to sign.
        </p>
        <p class="state">
          <strong>Being built.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <div class="more section">
      <section aria-labelledby="phone-heading">
        <h2 id="phone-heading" class="h3">
          On a phone or tablet
        </h2>
        <p>
          Yardulum runs in the web browser on a phone or tablet. Once a property
          is loaded, it opens again without a signal.
        </p>
        <p class="state">
          <strong>Works today.</strong>
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="sale-heading">
        <h2 id="sale-heading" class="h3">
          Built for the sale
        </h2>
        <p>
          Yardulum isn’t the software you bill or run crews with. It’s built for
          the sale itself, and it’s meant to work alongside the estimating and
          office software you already use.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section aria-labelledby="survey-heading">
        <h2 id="survey-heading" class="h3">
          Not a survey
        </h2>
        <p>
          Measurements come from public aerial photos and elevation data.
          They’re drafts for designing and pricing, not a survey. Confirm every
          quantity on site before your customer signs, and don’t use them to
          settle property lines.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <section class="section faq" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
      <h2 id="faq-heading">Common questions</h2>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I or my salespeople need CAD skills?</summary>
        <p>
          No. We’re building it for people who’ve never used CAD. Measuring on
          the model takes two taps, and Charles can show you.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I have to switch software?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Keep what you use. Yardulum is for the sale, and we’re building it
          to work alongside the software you bill and run crews with.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Who sees my prices?</summary>
        <p>
          Charles and Darian, the two owners. Your price list stays yours: we
          use it to build your estimates, and we don’t sell it. Your written
          terms will list every outside service that handles your data.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where do you work?</summary>
        <p>
          Northeast Ohio: Kent, Akron, Cleveland, Canton, Youngstown and the
          towns between. Charles sets up each company in person.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
    <section
      id="where-it-stands"
      class="section"
      aria-labelledby="stands-heading"
    >
      <h2 id="stands-heading">Where it stands</h2>
      <p class="small muted">
        Updated <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time>
      </p>
      <ul class="progress">
        <li>
          <strong>
            <time datetime="2026-10">October 2026</time>
          </strong>
          The property model and measuring work in the Yardulum app on our test
          properties.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>
            <time datetime="2026-10-23">October 23, 2026</time>
          </strong>
          Our first test model of a Kent, Ohio location, built from public
          elevation data and aerial photos.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Being built now</strong>
          Designing on the model and pricing from your own price list.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="close-heading">
      <h2 id="close-heading">See it in person</h2>
      <p>
        Call or text Charles. He’ll show you the property model and where the
        rest stands.
      </p>
      <CallActions />
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
<style>
  /* The figure column is 26rem (416 px at default text size). With enlarged text it stops at
     42% of the row, so the step text keeps a readable width, or at the 18.5rem the step 4
     proposal needs to show its Amount column, up to half the row. */
  .step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns:
      minmax(0, min(26rem, max(416px, 42%, min(18.5rem, 50%))))
      minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas: "fig head" "fig body";
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
    column-gap: var(--s12);
  }
  .step > h2 {
    grid-area: head;
  }
  /* PriceMath renders its own <figure>, so reach it with :global. */
  .step > :global(figure) {
    grid-area: fig;
  }
  .step > .step-body {
    grid-area: body;
  }
  /* Step 2 shows the design: keep its badge 2 and drop the model's badge 1, which step 1 already shows. */
  #step-2 + figure :global(.yd-b1) {
    display: none;
  }
  .step-body p {
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-top: var(--s4);
  }
  .step-body .state {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .more {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s8);
  }
  .more section {
    border-top: var(--stroke) solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: var(--s6);
  }
  .more p {
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: var(--s3);
  }
  .more .state {
    color: var(--text);
  }
  /* Each column draws its own rule, so the row's section rule would double it. */
  .more.section {
    border-top: 0;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .progress {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--s4) 0 0;
  }
  .progress li {
    padding-block: var(--s3);
    border-top: var(--stroke) solid var(--rule);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .progress strong {
    color: var(--text);
    display: block;
  }
  /* 761-900 px (iPad portrait): a fixed 416 px figure would squeeze the step text to about
     30 characters a line, so figure and text share the row equally. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .step {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      column-gap: var(--s8);
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .step {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-areas: "head" "fig" "body";
    }
    .step > :global(figure) {
      margin-top: var(--s5);
    }
    .more {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s6);
    }
  }
</style>
